Dave from a political activist  standpoint with what you know the  strategy of Colin Kaepernick did it work  with what he was setting out to do  absolutely and I love the way you asked  that question because I've been thinking  a lot dan about the action and then the  deed and there's so much focus on the  action the anthem question not standing  for it we're not really looking at the  deed which is the idea of trying to  start a broader discussion about  dissatisfaction with how issues of  police brutality and police violence are  handled in this country and if we look  back at the last year we've seen other  athletes almost overwhelmingly African  American athletes trying to push this  discussion more into the mainstream and  using their form of reports so LeBron  James Carmelo D Wade and Chris Paul in  it in this very scripted fashion at the  ESPYs and they largely got praised for  it for a couple of days and then we  forgot about it Colin Kaepernick took a  bold action he also took aside he didn't  just say we need to bring peace to the  community he said there's a problem  systemically and I'm going to stand up  against it and by taking a side he  certainly invited a lot more he invited  a lot more criticism he may even be  jeopardizing his career but if the goal  was to have this discussion in a more  full way then you have to say mission  accomplished what's the one overriding  question you have for Kaepernick Mike  well what are you willing to sacrifice  how far are you willing to go with this  and it seems like he's answered that but  I think it's about to get a lot hotter  than it is right now I think they're  they're open questions I'm sure you've  read them about whether he's going to  stay on the 49ers that could or could  not be for football reasons but then the  bigger question is will another team  take a flyer on what is an obviously  talented player at a position that's  bereft of talent and that will really  answer the question about NFL owners  about what matters more to them public  relations they're overwhelmingly  right-wing politics if you go by  political donations or winning games  because if the focus really is winning  games then you want Colin Kaepernick  and at least half the teams in this  league how would you feel if the NFL had  a rule that made you stand during the  anthem I think it would be the sort of  thing that we would have to point at  with our mouths wide open because this  idea of coercive patriotism to celebrate  freedom is just it is dissolved on the  face of it and this is something that a  lot of people have been arguing for many  many years it's just a discussion like  this that brings it to a flash point  like why do we do this in sports we  don't do it before movies most jobs  don't do that before you start standing  for the national anthem yet we do it in  sports and not only do we do it in  sports and we do it in a way that is  largely coercive that it's like you know  you are told to stand and players who  don't stand I tend to be called out for  it in very harsh terms even if there  aren't rules to that effect and I got to  take what it looks so funny about this  to me that I keep thinking about what  happened after nine eleven in Yankee  Stadium at the last year's of George  Steinbrenner when they started to put up  chains in the bleachers to keep people  from leaving the stands and going to the  bathroom during the god Bless America  seventh-inning stretch and these are in  the last days of steinbrenner and one  person actually sued through the ACLU  because he was actually thrown out of  the stadium for trying to step over the  chains that existed and just this idea  of using chains I mean not figurative  change in this case what literal chains  to get people to celebrate freedom it's  not the worst thing in the world for us  to question it's also not the worst  thing in the world the question about  likewise quartz is used as a method for  celebrating the military and I've spoken  to pat tillman's mother about this it's  interesting how many people have used  filming as a way to bash Colin  Kaepernick but this idea of using the  camaraderie of sports the joy that so  many people have felt in sports to say  to mostly work in class poor kids you  can find that similar camaraderie and  that similar joy in the military and  you're not going to find it at  McDonald's and the whole history of  recruitment and connections between the  Armed Forces and the Pentagon and the  NFL is its own sordid history which of  course is now being investigated in the  US Senate  if you go back to Muhammad Ali's passing  and we've talked about this with the  ESPYs with LeBron and company you  mentioned them I just wonder what impact  Ali has had and will continue to have  with a lot of these younger athletes who  were not alive to understand what he did  how he did it when he did it why he did  it but now they're learning about it I  don't know if anybody's ass Kaepernick  who was the inspiration like what they  when did the light go off that said I'm  glad when this you know that that's what  I would like to know is I just get a  feeling those guys at the ESPYs were  inspired by what Ali did and I don't  know if Colin Kaepernick was as well I  would be a great question for him I  think it's interesting that the  celebration of ollie I think there's a  lot of ways it's opened a can of worms  where it's like I think a lot of  athletes are saying well where we  celebrating him for and why and what is  the courage on display I gotta take one  of the things that's being left out and  that was left out of the lot of the  olive celebrations is that he was in the  1960s in the words of promoter Bob Arum  one of the great promoters in boxing  history a quote unquote dead piece of  merchandise and if you go back and think  a look at some of Ali's most famous  fights in the 60 the fights we celebrate  today there were empty seats all over  the arena again Miami in lewiston  against Sonny Liston people were voting  with their feet largely you know white  fans we're voting with their feet and  saying we're not going to celebrate this  radical we're going to actually turn our  back to him and I think Colin Kaepernick  is going to start facing some of that  and it's something that that LeBron Paul  etc ESPYs protest that kind of protest  doesn't engender that kind of backlash  one of the things that Kaepernick said  was he said I don't care if i lose  endorsements and i think that's kind of  like crossing the real Rubicon because i  think the endorsement cudgel is one of  the things that's kept a lot of athletes  in the line over the last 30 years good  stuff Dave good to talk to you again  thanks for joining us thank you sir Dave  siren he's the sports editor for the  nation website is edge of sports com  always appreciate his thoughts his  angles on this the Dan Patrick show  weekday mornings on audience  you
 

